TITLE
Software Design Practices in an Academic Setting

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT 

The Ptolemy Project has an extensive history of releasing freely
available software.  In the past, software development efforts in
academia have often relied upon the super-human efforts of the 'hero
programmer'.  When the star programmer leaves, software quality
sometimes suffers.

<p>Our group has developed a set of methods for training new software
developers, passing on good software practices and collaborating as a
team.

<p>Our primary tool is to have regularly scheduled study groups where we
cover new areas in topics such as software engineering.  Our early
study groups covered design patterns and Unified Modeling Language
(UML).  As we started to apply what we learned, we started having
regular design and code reviews of software under development.

<p>One outgrowth of the design and review system was to implement
a code rating system, where code that is less stable is red, 
code that has been design reviewed is yellow, and code
that has been design and code reviewed is green.

<p>The Ptolemy project has a long standing tradition of nightly builds,
but after studying development processes, we decided to make the build
results available to the developer community.   We also designed
and implemented a test suite and started measuring test case code coverage.

<p>Our initial work is documented in 
"<a href="http://ptolemy.eecs.berkeley.edu/publications/papers/99/sftwareprac/">Software Practice in the Ptolemy Project</a>"

<p>We are starting to transfer some of our tools to other groups in
academia and in industry.
